{
 "section": "07",
 "slug": "07-05-gobekli-tepe",
 "heading": "## 07 — #5 GÖBEKLI TEPE, TÜRKIYE",
 "text": "Number five: Göbekli Tepe, in south-eastern Türkiye. Its monumental enclosures date to roughly ninety-six hundred to eighty-two hundred B.C.E., around seven thousand years before the Great Pyramid. Giant T-shaped limestone pillars, some over five metres tall, stand in rings, carved with foxes, boars, snakes and vultures. Some even have arms and hands. The builders were hunter-gatherer communities, before fully established farming in the region. They quarried the pillars with stone tools. One unfinished pillar still lies in the bedrock. Why build it? Ritual and communal gathering are the leading interpretations, but the debate is open. Some researchers even suggest gatherings like these helped push people toward farming. Göbekli Tepe shows ambition. The next site shows precision, and more wild theories than almost anywhere on Earth.",
 "text_chars": 843,
 "previous_text": "But so far, every builder had farming behind them. The next site breaks that rule.",
 "next_text": "Number four: Puma Punku, part of the larger Tiwanaku complex near Lake Titicaca, in Bolivia. Its stones now lie toppled.",
